<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Giri Kedaton</h1><p class="st-meta">1487 CE – 1680 CE (approx.) · capital Giri (Gresik)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A line of Muslim priest-kings near Gresik in East Java, whose spiritual authority reached from Lombok to the Moluccas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-giri">Sunan Giri</a>, born Raden Paku and counted among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Songo</a>, founded a religious school on the hills of Giri near the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gresik">Gresik</a> in the late 15th century. His descendants ruled as both religious teachers and princes, with the title Prabu Satmata or Sunan Giri.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Giri was a centre of Islamic learning that trained students from across the archipelago, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zainal-abidin-of-ternate">Zainal Abidin of Ternate</a>. Its leaders blessed the coronation of Javanese rulers and sent preachers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-prapen">Sunan Prapen</a>, in the 16th century, was its most influential ruler. Its wealth came from pilgrims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gresik">Gresik's</a> trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung of Mataram</a> conquered Giri in 1636, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangeran-pekik">Pangeran Pekik</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-surabaya">Surabaya</a> so as not to fight the saints himself. Giri joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya-rebellion">Trunajaya rebellion</a> against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram</a> and was destroyed in 1680 by Mataram and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> forces. The tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-giri">Sunan Giri</a> remains a major pilgrimage site.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/giri-kedaton.jpg" alt="Giri Kedaton" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: H. A. Buchory Rachman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giri_Kedaton-Gapura.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1487 CE – 1680 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Giri (Gresik)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sunan Giri, Sunan Prapen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Giri, Kedaton Giri</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1584&amp;focus=giri-kedaton" aria-label="Giri Kedaton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7797 2446 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Giri Kedaton lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8117 2702L8052 2725"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8052 2725h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8117 2702h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8117 2702h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1584&amp;focus=giri-kedaton">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

